<Layout title="Linh Nguyen">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto pt-8 pb-16">
    <a href="/journals" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline"
      >&larr; Back to Journals</a
    >
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Crack Playlist
    </h1>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mb-8 italic">April 30, 2021</p>
    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-4">
      <p class="text-lg">
        There's been <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9842905/"
          >some</a
        >
        <a href="https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/2059204320965058"
          >research</a
        > at the intersection of music and psychology suggesting that the music we
        listen to as pre-teens and early adolescents establishes our music tastes
        for the rest of our lives.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Anecdotally, this is spot-on. Obviously this isn't to say that our music
        tastes are fixed after adolescence. There's evidence that we can learn
        to enjoy different types of music through <a
          href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mere-exposure_effect"
          >repeated exposure</a
        >
        (which is why it's valuable to listen to foreign music, music from different
        decades, musical genres we don't typically gravitate toward, etc).
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Still, I've been reflecting on the special relationship I have with the
        artists I listened to during my first substantial exposure to music. The
        songs are often objective garbage, and all follow the same
        songwriting/production formula, but sometimes they feel like the
        greatest music ever recorded. They hit like crack (figuratively
        speaking).
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Recently, [my brother] and I nearly pulled an all nighter compiling a
        collaborative playlist of the first music we ever listened to - the
        songs we heard on the radio while being driven to elementary school. It
        was so much harder than I thought it would be. Back then, we didn't yet
        care who was performing the music we listened to. There was also no
        Shazam or streaming sites to look up songs. If you missed the radio
        host's note on what a song was, you were out of luck. Throughout this
        exercise, there was a lot of "THAT was Maroon 5?" and "Wow, Kelly
        Clarkson again?" and "Whatever happened to [Leona Lewis / Matchbox
        Twenty / Hoobastank]?"
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        This was one of my most rewarding music-related experiences as of late.
        Even arguing about whether certain songs belonged on the playlist was
        fun. Rule #1: Songs had to have been played on 95.3 KUIC between 2003
        and 2010. Sometimes one of us would swear that a song belonged on there
        only to discover it was released during high school. Other times, I
        would push back against a song because I now associate it with a
        completely different period of life. We also had a fun debate over
        whether to use "Taylor's Version" editions of songs or her original
        releases.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        I started sharing this playlist a bit, but not everyone has the same
        connection to the music. (But when it hits, it HITS.) Some friends
        didn't start listening to music until later in life. Some older friends
        associate these songs with high school or college. Other friends didn't
        grow up in the US and don't recognize the songs. There's also the
        occasional stray 80's or 90's throwback that would play on our radio
        station but not our friends' radio stations.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        I get the sense that this project will be forever a work in progress.
        It's so hard to track down all the songs that are supposed to be here.
        Every once in a while, one of us will hear an early-2000's song over the
        grocery store speakers, and then text the other for approval to add it.
        There are so many one-hit-wonders and artists we've forgotten about that
        we'll probably be doing this for a long time.
      </p>

      <iframe
        style="border-radius:12px"
        src="https://open.spotify.com/embed/playlist/0O2zUFt779QgYK5ig9uhGv?utm_source=generator"
        width="100%"
        height="352"
        frameborder="0"
        allowfullscreen=""
        allow="autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; fullscreen; picture-in-picture"
        loading="lazy"></iframe>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
